---
description: Pass git commit messages and PR bodies via files, never heredocs
alwaysApply: true
---

# Never build a commit message with a heredoc

`git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF' … EOF)"` has hung this repo's agent shell
indefinitely. The wrapper layer can rewrite the command (e.g. injecting a
`--trailer` flag), and if the heredoc's delimiter is lost in that rewrite the
shell blocks forever waiting for input. git never runs, so there is no timeout
and no error — just silence.

## Multi-paragraph messages: write a file, point git at it

```bash
# ❌ BAD — hangs
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Subject line

Body paragraph.
EOF
)"

# ✅ GOOD — write the message with a file-writing tool, then:
git commit -F .git/COMMIT_MSG_DRAFT
rm -f .git/COMMIT_MSG_DRAFT
```

Draft inside `.git/` so the file is never picked up as a working-tree change.
Same rule for PR bodies: `gh pr create --body-file <path>`, never `--body "$(…)"`.

## Short messages: repeated -m

Each `-m` becomes its own paragraph, with no shell parsing to get wrong.

```bash
git commit -m "Short subject line" -m "Body explaining why."
```

## If a git command goes silent for ~30s

Check whether git ever started before assuming it is slow:

```bash
ls .git/index.lock   # absent  => git never took the lock
git log -1           # unchanged => no commit was created
```

No lock and no new commit means the shell is stuck, not git. Killing it is safe
and leaves the staged index intact — then redo it with `-F`.
